{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790307378,"nonce":"zk1xJ9YR+HsXt79INp3N4Zx9/Ek=","message":"48266ea167514e3504757de251f0d2490505c5ac: bench_memory_probe runs each kernel on one probe thread per test and waits 120 s for it (every kernel of a full probe pass returns in a small fraction of that on the Mac); a kernel that does not return fails the test with its name, and the binary exits rather than holding the job. Forced on the Mac with a 1 ms limit: the test failed at once naming bench_any_cast. Reading the llm_decode path for Windows hazards: runtime threads set explicit 16 to 64 MB stacks; the probe thread and the band pool take RUST_MIN_STACK like libtest's threads; parallel dispatch is off by default so the band pool is not in play; the interpreter caches process symbols; module paths are bundled. One clear hazard fixed in cf8bcbc5614a6fa9f682f09d8b820d4e45cbce54 (341fed4): Cranelift emitted no stack probes on Windows, so a frame over a page could jump the guard page. That faults rather than hangs, so it is not shown to be this hang. Still open: llm_decode is the first kernel in sorted order with f32 parameters (fast_exp, inv_rms, scale_by with an f32 between integers), a 9-argument call (attend, past the 4 Windows register arguments) and f32 loop-carried values through OSR; the interpreter-to-native thunk, OSR entry and stack-argument layout under WindowsFastcall are where to look next. Needs the next Windows run's watchdog message, then ZYNTAX_DISABLE_OSR=1 and ZYNTAX_TRACE_TIER_UP=1 on that kernel alone.","files":null}]}